# CSMS Project Lifecycle Framework
### Aligned with UNECE R155 & ISO/SAE 21434

> Created by Maya Sarwade | Cybersecurity PM | FEV India  
> Based on real-world CSMS implementation experience across 10+ vehicle programs

---

## What is a CSMS?

A **Cybersecurity Management System (CSMS)** is an organisation-level framework that an automotive OEM or supplier must implement and maintain to demonstrate that cybersecurity is systematically managed across the entire vehicle lifecycle — from concept through decommissioning.

UNECE Regulation 155 (R155) mandates that all passenger vehicles, light commercial vehicles, trucks, and buses sold in UNECE member countries must be manufactured by organisations holding a valid **CSMS Certificate of Compliance**.

---

## CSMS Project Phases

```
Phase 1: Gap Analysis
    ↓
Phase 2: CSMS Design & Policy Development
    ↓
Phase 3: Process Implementation
    ↓
Phase 4: Supplier Management Integration
    ↓
Phase 5: Internal Audit & Review
    ↓
Phase 6: Technical Service Assessment
    ↓
Phase 7: Type Approval Authority Approval
    ↓
Phase 8: Ongoing Monitoring & Continuous Improvement
```

---

## Phase 1 — Gap Analysis

**Objective:** Assess current organisational cybersecurity posture against UNECE R155 and ISO/SAE 21434 requirements.

**Key Activities:**
- Review existing cybersecurity policies, processes, and documentation
- Map current practices against UNECE R155 Annex 5 requirements
- Identify gaps in people, process, and technology
- Produce a Gap Analysis Report with priority rankings

**Typical Duration:** 4–8 weeks

**Key Deliverables:**
- [ ] Gap Analysis Report
- [ ] Cybersecurity Maturity Assessment Score
- [ ] Remediation Roadmap with timeline and ownership

**Stakeholders:** CISO / Security Lead, Quality Manager, Engineering Leads, Procurement

---

## Phase 2 — CSMS Design & Policy Development

**Objective:** Design the CSMS framework and develop all required policies and procedures.

**Key Activities:**
- Define organisational cybersecurity roles and responsibilities
- Develop Cybersecurity Policy (top-level)
- Develop supporting procedures: incident response, vulnerability management, supplier management, risk management
- Define cybersecurity objectives and KPIs

**Typical Duration:** 6–10 weeks

**Key Deliverables:**
- [ ] Cybersecurity Policy (signed by top management)
- [ ] Cybersecurity Roles & Responsibilities Matrix (RACI)
- [ ] Incident Response Procedure
- [ ] Vulnerability Management Procedure
- [ ] Cybersecurity Risk Management Procedure
- [ ] Supplier Cybersecurity Requirements Document

---

## Phase 3 — Process Implementation

**Objective:** Embed CSMS processes into day-to-day operations and vehicle development workflows.

**Key Activities:**
- Integrate cybersecurity into product development processes (concept, development, production)
- Implement cybersecurity case management for each vehicle type
- Establish TARA (Threat Analysis & Risk Assessment) process
- Implement cybersecurity testing and validation gates
- Train relevant personnel on new processes

**Typical Duration:** 8–16 weeks

**Key Deliverables:**
- [ ] Cybersecurity Plan (per vehicle program)
- [ ] TARA Reports (per vehicle type/component)
- [ ] Cybersecurity Cases (per vehicle program)
- [ ] Training Completion Records
- [ ] Process Integration Evidence

---

## Phase 4 — Supplier Management Integration

**Objective:** Extend CSMS requirements to Tier 1 and Tier 2 suppliers.

**Key Activities:**
- Develop Supplier Cybersecurity Requirements specification
- Distribute requirements to all relevant suppliers
- Assess supplier cybersecurity capabilities
- Establish supplier monitoring and reporting cadence
- Include cybersecurity clauses in supplier contracts

**Typical Duration:** 6–12 weeks (ongoing)

**Key Deliverables:**
- [ ] Supplier Cybersecurity Requirements Document
- [ ] Supplier Cybersecurity Assessment Questionnaire
- [ ] Supplier Capability Assessment Results
- [ ] Supplier Cybersecurity Agreement / Contract Addendum
- [ ] Supplier Monitoring Plan

---

## Phase 5 — Internal Audit & Review

**Objective:** Verify CSMS implementation effectiveness before external assessment.

**Key Activities:**
- Conduct internal audit against UNECE R155 Annex 5 checklist
- Review all CSMS documentation for completeness and accuracy
- Conduct management review of cybersecurity objectives and KPIs
- Address all non-conformances identified
- Prepare evidence packages for Technical Service assessment

**Typical Duration:** 4–6 weeks

**Key Deliverables:**
- [ ] Internal Audit Report
- [ ] Non-Conformance Log with closure evidence
- [ ] Management Review Minutes
- [ ] CSMS Evidence Package

---

## Phase 6 — Technical Service Assessment

**Objective:** Independent assessment by an approved Technical Service (e.g., TÜV SÜD, TÜV Rheinland, DEKRA).

**Key Activities:**
- Submit CSMS documentation package to Technical Service
- Respond to Technical Service queries and document requests
- Facilitate on-site assessment (interviews, process walkthroughs)
- Address any findings raised during assessment

**Typical Duration:** 4–8 weeks

**Key Deliverables:**
- [ ] Technical Service Assessment Report
- [ ] Finding Responses and Closure Evidence
- [ ] CSMS Assessment Certificate (upon successful completion)

---

## Phase 7 — Type Approval Authority Approval

**Objective:** Obtain CSMS Type Approval from the national authority (e.g., DGTD in India, KBA in Germany, VCA in UK).

**Key Activities:**
- Submit Technical Service Assessment Certificate to Type Approval Authority
- Provide additional documentation as required
- Receive CSMS Certificate of Compliance

**Typical Duration:** 2–6 weeks (varies by country)

**Key Deliverables:**
- [ ] CSMS Certificate of Compliance
- [ ] Vehicle Type Approval Certificate (per vehicle type)

---

## Phase 8 — Ongoing Monitoring & Continuous Improvement

**Objective:** Maintain CSMS effectiveness across the product lifecycle and improve continuously.

**Key Activities:**
- Monitor cybersecurity threats and vulnerabilities relevant to fielded vehicles
- Manage cybersecurity incidents and report to authorities as required
- Conduct periodic internal audits (typically annual)
- Update CSMS documentation in response to regulatory changes
- Renew CSMS certificate (typically every 3 years)

**Key Deliverables (ongoing):**
- [ ] Incident Reports (as required)
- [ ] Annual Internal Audit Reports
- [ ] CSMS Review Records
- [ ] Regulatory Update Log

